==============================================================================
D. Sign-consistency check: target Sec5.1's delta(x) formula vs the Lemma
==============================================================================
Independent integrating-factor re-derivation of the Lemma's sign:
 ODE: u'(x) - (x+Y)u(x) = f(x).
 Integrating factor mu(x) = exp(-(x^2/2+xY)) satisfies
 d/dx[mu*u] = mu*f(x).  Integrate x to X->infinity, use u bounded
 and mu(X)->0 (since -(X^2/2+XY)->-infinity for Y>=0):
   0 - mu(x)u(x) = int_x^inf mu(t) f(t) dt
   u(x) = -exp(x^2/2+xY) * int_x^inf exp(-(t^2/2+tY)) f(t) dt
 ==> the leading MINUS sign is forced, confirmed independently of
 the Leibniz-rule proof in Part A/B.

CONCLUSION: target's Sec5.1 formula
  delta(x) = int_0^inf e^{-u^2/2-u(x+y1)} f(x+u) du
is missing the leading minus sign relative to a correct application
of the Growth-Exclusion Lemma (as independently re-derived here AND
as the target's OWN Sec2.1 correctly applies it to (E1) itself).
SEVERITY ASSESSMENT: since the very next step takes |delta(x)| and
bounds |f(x+u)|, |-Z|=|Z| for any Z, so the missing sign does NOT
propagate to the final oscillation bound (star-star) -- it is a
self-contained, non-consequential algebra slip in Sec5.1's exposed
intermediate formula. Flagged as LOW severity.

